    <main id="main" class="page-width article">
      <header class="article-intro">
        <p class="location">White Plains, New York</p>
        <h1>How Westminster Ridge got its lake</h1>
        <p class="intro">A hundred and twenty years ago this hillside was one man's land: open fields and woods, with no streets and no lake. This is the story of how it became a neighborhood, told through the maps, magazines, and photographs that survive.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-wide">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1910 map in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1910-white-plains-map.jpg" width="1536" height="999" alt="1910 map of White Plains and the surrounding area" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>White Plains in 1910. Westminster Ridge would grow up near the eastern edge of this map. <span class="credit">Westchester County atlas.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </header>

      <section id="land" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="land-title">
        <h2 id="land-title">Mr. Todd's land</h2>
        <p>For most of its history White Plains was farm country. The Weckquaesgeek called the area Quarropas, usually translated as "white marshes." English settlers bought the land in 1683, and as late as 1820 only about 675 people lived here. That began to change in 1844, when the railroad arrived from New York City.</p>
        <p>By 1901 the hillside that would become Westminster Ridge belonged to Arthur C. Todd. The county atlas from that year prints his name, "A. C. Todd," across a large tract of open land. There are no streets on it yet, and no lake.</p>
        <p>Ten years later the map is different. The 1911 atlas shows a long body of water at the foot of the hill, labeled "RESERVOIR," with what looks like a small dam at one end. Nearby is a new name: Westminster Park. Local histories say Todd laid out Westminster Park around 1912, and that the pond still carries his name. It is Todds Pond on maps today.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1901 A. C. Todd map in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1901-ac-todd.jpg" width="1200" height="1120" alt="1901 county atlas detail labeled A. C. Todd" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>1901. The tract is marked "A. C. Todd." No lake appears. <span class="credit">Westchester County Archives.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1911 reservoir map in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1911-reservoir.jpg" width="1800" height="1250" alt="1911 county atlas showing a reservoir and a dam-like feature" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>1911. A "RESERVOIR" has appeared, with a dam drawn at one end. <span class="credit">Westchester County Archives.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Between those two maps, someone made or enlarged the pond. We don't yet know who built it or whether they started from a spring, a stream, or a wet meadow. But it was there before any of the houses.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="park" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="park-title">
        <h2 id="park-title">Westminster Park, 1917</h2>
        <p>In January 1917 a New York real-estate journal announced a plan for 52 houses at Westminster Park, White Plains, at a total cost of about $200,000. In July the same journal listed the first of them: a two-and-a-half-story frame and stucco house costing $10,000. It named the people behind it. Norman W. Walker was president of the Westminster Park Company, Joseph J. Eberle was the architect, and M. W. Walker was the builder.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the January 1917 Westminster Park notice in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1917-january-notice.jpg" width="580" height="710" alt="January 1917 real-estate notice for Westminster Park" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>January 1917: 52 houses planned. <span class="credit">Real Estate Record and Builders' Guide.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the July 1917 Westminster Park notice in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1917-july-notice.jpg" width="592" height="850" alt="July 1917 real-estate notice naming Westminster Park participants" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>July 1917: the first house, and the men building it. <span class="credit">Real Estate Record and Builders' Guide.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Three months after the first notice, the United States entered the First World War, and building slowed across the country. We don't know how many of the 52 houses went up. In 1919 a sale map titled "Subdivision of property of A. C. Todd" was filed with the county, dividing the rest of the land into lots. It would take another developer to fill them.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="stafford" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="stafford-title">
        <h2 id="stafford-title">Fred Stafford's hilltop</h2>
        <p>That developer was Fred P. Stafford, the president of a local laundry. Around 1927 he began selling lots under a new name, Westminster Ridge. He kept six lots at the top of the hill and built his own house there. Roads went in over the next two years, following the slope of the land instead of a grid.</p>
        <p>Stafford's neighborhood was expensive. By 1930 about 30 houses stood on the hill, most selling for $15,000 to $40,000. In 1934 the city renamed Colonial Place in his honor. It has been Stafford Place ever since.</p>
        <p>According to a 1939 magazine article, the property "was once an estate," and the original house was still standing. Nobody has yet proven which house that is. The strongest candidate is 20 Stafford Place, a large stucco Colonial on nearly three-quarters of an acre. Property records date it to 1907, before Westminster Park existed, and it is on the street named for Stafford.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-wide">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the map showing 20 Stafford Place in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/20-stafford-map-lead.jpg" width="1800" height="1600" alt="Map showing an approximate marker near 20 Stafford Place and the reservoir" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>The red marker shows where 20 Stafford Place sits today. We added it; it is not on the original map.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="perry" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="perry-title">
        <h2 id="perry-title">The Depression, and a second start</h2>
        <p>Then the market collapsed. Through the early 1930s almost nothing was built on the ridge. In the two years before 1938, only two or three lots sold. The roads and utilities were in, but about 80 lots sat empty.</p>
        <p>In 1938 an architect and builder named Lee Perry bought all of them through his firm, the County Housing Corporation. His idea was to build good houses that cost much less than Stafford's. Perry drew the plans himself and fitted each house to its lot on the steep hillside. His first house went up in April 1938. By September, 41 were finished or under construction, and sales were running ahead of the builders.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-half">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1939 Westminster Ridge plot plan in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1939-plot-plan.jpg" width="467" height="570" alt="1939 plot plan showing winding roads, homes, woods, and the lake" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>Perry's plan for Westminster Ridge, with the lake at the center. <span class="credit">Architectural Forum, March 1939.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p id="house">Buyers chose from a few standard designs. The Lee was the smallest, with a downstairs lavatory and good closets. The Greenfield had five rooms, a dining alcove, and an attached garage. The Westminster was designed for the wooded hillside lots. Prices ran from $5,800 to $8,500.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1938 house types page in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1938-house-types.jpg" width="1172" height="730" alt="1938 page showing Westminster Ridge house photographs and floor plans" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>The Lee, The Westminster, and The Greenfield, with floor plans. <span class="credit">American Builder, November 1938.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1938 house photographs in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1938-houses.jpg" width="1172" height="520" alt="1938 photographs of two Westminster Ridge houses" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>Two new houses in 1938, priced at $5,975 and $7,950. <span class="credit">American Builder, November 1938.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="lake" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="lake-title">
        <h2 id="lake-title">The neighbors buy the lake</h2>
        <p>Perry's advertisements promised "a small lake and playground." The lake still belonged to the original developers, though. So the new residents formed a lake club and set out to buy it themselves.</p>
        <p>In 1939 membership cost $2 a month. The dues paid for equipment, sand for the beach, and a lifeguard. Admission fees from members went toward the purchase price. It is the earliest record we have of what is now the Lakenridge Club.</p>
        <p>A county aerial photograph taken on December 19, 1940 shows the result: a finished neighborhood of curving streets and new roofs, with the pond in the middle.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1940 aerial photograph in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1940-aerial.jpg" width="900" height="990" alt="1940 aerial photograph showing the pond among neighborhood roads and houses" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>December 1940, from the air. <span class="credit">Westchester County historical aerial collection.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the Lakenridge Club letter in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/lakenridge-letter.png" width="935" height="1210" alt="Lakenridge Club membership letter describing the lake season" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>A recent Lakenridge membership letter. Swimming, fishing, and the spring cleanup are still the center of the season.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>The club has been run by neighbors ever since. It received its federal tax-exempt status as a recreation club in 1973. Pasquale "Pat" Barile, a longtime White Plains civic figure, served as its president for many years. Today it still hires lifeguards, runs a swim team and a kids' fishing derby, and opens each season with a lake cleanup.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="streets" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="streets-title">
        <h2 id="streets-title">The streets that were never built</h2>
        <p>Old maps of the neighborhood show more streets than exist today. The 1929–31 county atlas includes Bernard Place and Francis Place north of the pond, and a road called Stang Avenue running north from Colonial Place.</p>
        <div class="image-grid image-grid-2">
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the 1929 to 1931 street atlas in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1930-reservoir.jpg" width="2600" height="2300" alt="1929 to 1931 county atlas showing Bernard Place, Francis Place, Colonial Place, Stang Avenue, and the reservoir" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>1929–31. The planned streets around the reservoir. <span class="credit">Westchester County Archives.</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure class="figure">
            <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the present-day street map in a larger view">
              <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/today-pond-map.png" width="768" height="768" alt="Present-day map showing Chadwick Road, Stafford Place, Gilbert Place, and Todds Pond" loading="lazy" />
              <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
            </button>
            <figcaption>The same area today. Bernard Place and Francis Place are gone.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <p>Most of the old names are still here under new ones. Colonial Place was built around 1908. In 1934 it was briefly called Chadwick Place, then renamed Stafford Place that September. The road now called Chadwick Road started as Colonial Terrace. Its northern extension, added around 1927, was first named Stang Avenue. In 1934 the whole road became Chadwick Road.</p>
        <p>Bernard Place and Francis Place were never built. They stayed on the city map as "paper streets," and Chadwick Road stops short of where Bernard Place would have been. In 2012 the city moved to buy the woods between Chadwick and Bernard as a wildlife corridor and connected them to city-owned land along Francis Place. The streets that were never built became open space instead.</p>
        <p class="source-line">Street history from <a href="https://wpstreets.weebly.com/home/stafford-place" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Streets of White Plains ↗</a> and the <a href="https://dailyvoice.com/article/white-plains-to-buy-wildlife-corridor-for-130k/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daily Voice ↗</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="people" class="story-section" aria-labelledby="people-title">
        <h2 id="people-title">Who grew up here</h2>
        <p>Most of the people who lived on the ridge left little public record, but a few did. Stephen Holden III was born in White Plains and raised in Westminster Ridge. He became a local lawyer and civic leader who worked for historic preservation in the city. In 2009 the New York State Senate passed a resolution honoring his work, and it mentions the neighborhood by name.</p>
        <figure class="figure figure-half">
          <button class="image-trigger" type="button" aria-label="Open the resolution honoring Stephen Holden III in a larger view">
            <img src="/public/lakenridge/images/holden-resolution.jpg" width="910" height="690" alt="2009 legislative resolution honoring Stephen Holden III" loading="lazy" />
            <span class="image-hint">Click to enlarge</span>
          </button>
          <figcaption>The 2009 Senate resolution for Stephen Holden III. <span class="credit">New York State Senate.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>If you have old photographs of the pond, the beach, or the houses going up, or stories from people who remember the early lake club, we would like to hear from you. Much of this history is still in attics and family albums.</p>
      </section>

